  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:36px">
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="article">
        <p class="lede">Onshore, nearshore, and offshore are not three prices for the same thing. They are three different working relationships, with different overlap, control, and management demands. Choosing well starts with understanding what each model actually gives you, and what it asks of you.</p>

        <h2>The three models, defined plainly</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Onshore:</strong> hiring inside your own country. For a US business, a US employee. Full overlap, highest cost, familiar legal ground.</li>
          <li><strong>Nearshore:</strong> hiring in a nearby country with a small time difference. For US businesses, that usually means Latin America, with 0 to 3 hours of difference and near-total working-day overlap.</li>
          <li><strong>Offshore:</strong> hiring in a distant region, typically Asia or parts of Eastern Europe, with an 8 to 12 hour time difference. The lowest rates, and collaboration that runs either on shifted hours or asynchronously.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>One clarification that saves confusion later: a dedicated offshore or nearshore professional can still work your exact business hours. Professionals in the Philippines, for example, routinely work US shifts. The model's label describes geography; the schedule is a separate decision. What changes with distance is how much natural overlap exists when neither side shifts.</p>

        <h2>Head to head comparison</h2>
        <table>
          <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Onshore</th><th>Nearshore</th><th>Offshore</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical cost vs US hire</td><td>Baseline (highest)</td><td>40 to 60% less</td><td>50 to 70% less</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Time zone difference (US)</td><td>0 to 3 hours</td><td>0 to 3 hours</td><td>8 to 12 hours</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Real-time collaboration</td><td>Full day</td><td>Most or all of the day</td><td>Limited unless hours are shifted</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cultural and language alignment</td><td>Native</td><td>Strong, especially LATAM for US teams</td><td>Varies by country; Philippines strongest on English</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Talent pool depth</td><td>Limited to local market</td><td>Large</td><td>Largest</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Compliance complexity</td><td>Low, familiar</td><td>Medium, partner usually handles</td><td>Medium to high, partner usually handles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Presence-based and leadership roles</td><td>Collaborative roles needing live overlap</td><td>Defined, process-driven roles and shifted-hours coverage</td></tr>
        </table>

        <h2>When onshore is worth its price</h2>
        <p>Onshore wins when the work is inseparable from place: physical operations, in-person client delivery, regulated on-site requirements, or senior leadership that shapes culture face to face. It also wins when a role's output is so collaborative and unstructured that any friction costs more than the salary saving. Few back-office roles meet that bar, which is why the <a href="../../blog/remote-team-vs-in-house-cost/">loaded cost comparison</a> favors remote models for most operational hiring.</p>

        <h2>When nearshore fits best</h2>
        <p>Nearshore is the sweet spot for roles that live in meetings and shared working hours: customer-facing support during your business day, sales support, project coordination, and collaborative marketing or product work. You give up a little of offshore's price advantage and gain a team that is simply awake when you are, with strong cultural alignment for US customers. Speed to hire is typically 1 to 3 weeks through an established partner.</p>

        <h2>When offshore fits best</h2>
        <p>Offshore delivers the deepest talent pools and the strongest savings, and it excels at well-defined, process-driven work: data operations, bookkeeping, back-office processing, and support coverage that extends beyond your business day. The Philippines in particular combines near-native English with a mature remote-work culture. The management requirement is real, though: offshore rewards businesses that document processes and communicate in writing, the same discipline described in our <a href="../../blog/how-to-hire-remote-employees/">guide to hiring remote employees</a>. Businesses that skip documentation struggle in every model; offshore just shows it sooner.</p>

        <h2>The hybrid pattern most growing businesses land on</h2>
        <p>In practice, the answer is rarely one model. A common, effective structure is:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Onshore core:</strong> leadership, strategy, and client-facing senior roles</li>
          <li><strong>Nearshore:</strong> live-hours collaboration roles, support and sales during the US day</li>
          <li><strong>Offshore:</strong> process-driven operations, finance support, and extended coverage</li>
        </ul>
        <p>All three report into the same managers and work in the same tools. The model question is per role, not per company. Our <a href="../../services/staffing-recruitment/">staffing and recruitment</a> team maps roles to models during the discovery process, and our dedicated professionals work your business hours regardless of which region they are hired from.</p>

        <h2>A simple decision framework</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Does the role require physical presence or location-bound clearance? If yes, onshore.</li>
          <li>Does the role need 6 or more hours of live overlap with your team every day? If yes, nearshore, or offshore with shifted hours.</li>
          <li>Is the work process-driven with written inputs and measurable outputs? If yes, offshore gives the best economics.</li>
          <li>Still unsure? Start with one role, measure for 90 days, then expand the model that worked. Cost context for each route is in our <a href="../../blog/virtual-assistant-cost/">virtual assistant cost guide</a>.</li>
        </ol>
      </article>
